{
  "fromId": "e",
  "toId": "j_glide",
  "preamble": "A front mid vowel /e/ in hiatus before another vowel loses its syllabicity and becomes the palatal glide [j]. This yod formation was a major Vulgar Latin change (condemned in the Appendix Probi as 'vinea non vinia') that fed later Romance palatalizations; Medieval Greek synizesis did the same to [ˈea], [ˈeo], shifting the stress onto the second vowel (ἐννέα > ἐννιά, θεός > [ˈθjos]).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Glide Formation, Desyllabification, Hiatus Resolution",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Vulgar Latin",
      "languageFamily": "Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "vinea 'vineyard'",
          "to": "vinia [ˈwinja]",
          "note": "Appendix Probi: 'uinea non uinia'; the yod later palatalizes the nasal: Italian vigna, French vigne, Spanish viña."
        },
        {
          "from": "cavea 'cage'",
          "to": "cavia",
          "note": "Appendix Probi: 'cauea non cauia'."
        },
        {
          "from": "lancea 'lance'",
          "to": "lancia",
          "note": "Appendix Probi: 'lancea non lancia'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Medieval Greek",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Koine Greek ἐννέα /enˈne.a/ 'nine'",
          "to": "ἐννιά [eˈɲa]",
          "note": "Synizesis: stress moved to the second vowel and the first became [j], which then palatalized the preceding nasal."
        },
        {
          "from": "Koine Greek θεός /θeˈos/ 'god'",
          "to": "[ˈθjos]",
          "note": "Vernacular pronunciation shown in transcriptions of 12th-century texts; learned speech resisted synizesis."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
